          "text": "The attic was not silent that night. Though the lantern’s glow was steady, the air itself seemed to hum, vibrating faintly as if the walls carried a heartbeat. Charlotte lifted the lantern higher, her hand trembling, and the light caught the edges of the old chest. Emma followed close behind, her eyes wide with curiosity, while Lila skipped a step, eager as always, her excitement filling the dusty air. The chest sat in its usual place, but tonight it seemed alive, humming softly, as though something inside had been waiting for them.\n\nCharlotte knelt down, her breath shallow, and lifted the lid. Inside, the golden pen lay across the open book. Its glow was brighter than ever, pulsing faintly like a living thing. Emma leaned closer, her breath catching in her throat. “It’s waiting,” she whispered, her voice trembling with awe.\n\nBefore anyone could touch it, the pen lifted on its own. Slowly, carefully, it hovered above the page. Then, with deliberate strokes, it began to write. The scratching sound echoed through the attic, sharp and steady, louder than it should have been. Shimmering marks appeared, twisting into strange symbols none of them recognized, glowing faintly in the lantern light.\n\nLila gasped, clapping her hands. “It’s writing without us!” she cried, delighted. But Charlotte pulled her back quickly, her voice firm. “Don’t get too close,” she warned. The symbols glowed faintly, shifting as if they were alive, rearranging themselves into patterns that seemed to breathe.\n\nEmma’s eyes followed the lines, her brow furrowed. “They look like letters, but not in any language I know.” She reached out, her fingers trembling, but the pen darted away from her hand, scratching faster, as though it didn’t want to be interrupted.\n\nThe attic seemed to lean closer, listening. Dust swirled in the lantern light, forming shapes that almost looked like faces. The air grew colder, and the floorboards creaked beneath their feet. Charlotte’s heart thudded in her chest, her breath shallow.\n\nFinally, the pen stopped. The symbols shimmered one last time before settling into silence. The glow dimmed, but the words remained, etched into the page like a secret waiting to be discovered.\n\nEmma whispered, “It’s alive.” Her voice was barely audible, but the attic seemed to hear her. The lantern flickered, shadows stretching long across the walls.\n\nCharlotte closed the chest slowly, her voice barely above a whisper. “The book was never the only mystery. The pen has secrets too.”\n\nThe attic groaned, as if agreeing, and the three girls stood frozen, knowing that something had awakened — something that would not easily sleep again."
