  replaceKeeperPassageOccurrence(6, `The attic trembled as though it were alive, the ink swirling violently, filling every corner with its golden glow. Charlotte gripped the pen tightly, her hand trembling, but she refused to let go. Emma and Lila stood beside her, their breaths shallow, their eyes wide with fear. The Keeper’s shadow loomed larger than ever, his voice echoing through the attic like thunder. You cannot erase me, he said, his words heavy and suffocating. Charlotte’s heart raced, but she pressed the pen to the page, determined to prove him wrong.

The ink resisted, twisting violently, trying to push the pen away. But Charlotte’s hand didn’t falter. She scratched across the paper, forming words that glowed faintly. Emma placed her hand over Charlotte’s, steadying her, her voice trembling but firm. “We’ll write together,” she whispered. Lila joined them, her eyes blazing with determination. The pen glowed brighter, its light pulsing like a heartbeat, burning through the page.`, 1, `All three hands remained clasped around the golden pen. Emma’s hand still steadied Charlotte’s, and Lila’s fingers still held them both. None of them had let go since their first truths changed the living attic. Across the rolling floor, the Keeper gathered his shadow into the shape of a cloak.

You have borrowed my pen long enough, he said. Now write what I command.

The nib lurched beneath their joined grip. Together they tried to write: We choose what comes next. Before the sentence was complete, a black stroke hooked through choose and bent it into lose. The page now read: We lose what comes next. Their hands recoiled as one, but their grip held.

Lila caught the change first. “He is twisting the middle,” she warned.

Emma leaned so close that the gold light shone in her eyes. “Then make every word clear.” She spoke slowly while all three guided the pen: We are frightened, and we are still here. The sentence settled into the paper with a warm, steady glow. It did not pretend they felt fearless. It told the truth, and the ink could not bend it.

Lila chose the next words. “We make mistakes, and we can put them right.” Charlotte repeated them, Emma tested them, and together they wrote them down. The mark around Lila’s wrist cooled from burning gold to the warmth of sunlight. Charlotte added one more truth: No single voice speaks for all of us.

The new lines rose from the book like ribbons of light. They wound around the girls’ joined hands without separating them, and the ghostly faces surrounding the table turned away from the friends and towards the Keeper. Charlotte understood then that the magic did not answer only to bravery. It answered to honesty.`);

  addKeeperPassage(6, "Charlotte nodded, her heart racing. “We’ll finish this,” she whispered.", `A thread of black ink snapped around the pen and tugged it toward the Keeper. Charlotte’s hand slid across the page. Emma braced her elbow against the table while Lila caught Charlotte around the waist. The three of them were pulled forward an inch, then another. The open book waited beneath them like a deep golden pool.

  “Names,” Emma gasped. “Write our names. It cannot steal people it cannot name truthfully.”

  Charlotte did not know whether that rule was real, but it sounded like the kind of truth they could make real together. With her remaining strength she wrote Charlotte. Emma guided the pen through her own name. Lila guided all four letters of hers. Each name rang through the attic like a small bell.

  Their copied shadows split away from the walls. The false Charlottes, Emmas, and Lilas turned to mist because they had no histories behind their names—no summer jokes, no shared secrets, no memory of climbing the attic stairs together. The real girls remained.

  Lila laughed once, breathlessly. “He knows our fears, but he doesn’t know us.”

  “Then we remind him,” Charlotte said. She pulled the pen back over the page.`);

  addKeeperPassage(6, "Charlotte’s hand didn’t stop. “We’re not erasing you,” she whispered. “We’re ending you.”", `The Keeper changed his attack. His voice softened until it sounded almost kind. He reminded Charlotte of every warning she had ignored, Emma of every moment she had doubted her friends, and Lila of the wish that had first let the attic’s magic twist out of control. Shame pressed harder than the shadows had.

  Charlotte’s writing slowed. The worst part was that his words contained pieces of truth.

  Emma saw it too. “An ending does not have to say we were perfect,” she said. “It only has to say what we chose after we were wrong.”

  Lila’s eyes filled, but she lifted her chin. “I was careless,” she told the Keeper. “That is not the same as belonging to you.”

  Charlotte wrote their answers into the book. We listened. We learned. We came back for one another. With each sentence, the weight around them loosened. Their mistakes remained part of the story, but the Keeper could no longer use them as chains. He had offered them shame as a final chapter. Together, they turned it into a lesson and kept writing.`);

  addKeeperPassage(6, "Charlotte’s eyes blazed, her voice firm. “Not this one,” she said.", `Beyond the Keeper, a blank page lifted upright. It was clean, bright, and frightening in a different way. No warning had been written there. No command waited for them to obey. It offered choice, and choice meant responsibility.

  “What should the ending promise?” Charlotte asked.

  Emma considered the question even while loose pages spun around her. “That no one person owns a story.”

  “That being scared does not make you weak,” Lila added.

  Charlotte nodded. “And that magic must never make the choice for us.”

  They spoke the promises once, then again, until the words matched. Charlotte began their shared paragraph. Emma corrected a sentence the Keeper tried to turn backward. Lila guarded the margins, crossing out every command that crawled towards their work. The marks on their wrists joined in a thin circle of gold, not a chain now but a line connecting three hands.

  The Keeper rushed at them, cloak spreading from beam to beam. They did not look up. Their attention stayed on the page and on one another. For the first time, the Keeper was not the centre of the story. He was only the problem the three friends had decided to solve.`);

  replaceKeeperPassageOccurrence(6, "The attic trembled, the ink swirling violently, forming shapes that dissolved into dust. The pen glowed brighter, its light pulsing like a heartbeat, burning through the page. Emma’s voice broke, her hands trembling. “It’s working,” she whispered. Lila’s eyes blazed, her voice firm. “Keep going,” she said. Charlotte nodded, her heart racing. “We’ll finish this,” she whispered.", 2, `The Keeper sent one more wave of ink across the book. It did not rise into a monster or a memory. It flattened into a black line and covered the paragraph the girls had fought to write.

Emma stared at the blot. Then she noticed a pinprick of gold beneath it. “Covered is not the same as gone,” she said.

Lila angled the lantern until its beam skimmed the page. Their words shone through the darkness, faint but complete. Charlotte wrote beside them: Truth remains true even when someone tries to hide it. Emma added her name below the sentence, Lila added hers, and Charlotte placed her signature beneath both.

The black line cracked into hundreds of tiny flakes. They lifted harmlessly from the paper and vanished before reaching the floor. Across the attic, the Keeper’s shadow split along the same glowing seams. His voice no longer filled the room. It came from far away, thin and uncertain.

“It’s working,” Emma said. This time her voice did not break.

Lila tightened her hand over Charlotte’s. “Keep going.”

Charlotte guided the pen towards an open space. “We’ll finish this.”`);

  replaceKeeperPassageOccurrence(6, "The lantern flared, its flame burning brighter, casting long shadows across the walls. The attic roared, alive with twisted magic, but the girls didn’t stop. Their words glowed brighter, burning through the attic’s silence, rewriting the story itself. The Keeper’s shadow screamed, his voice echoing through the attic. You cannot erase me, he said, his words heavy and suffocating. Charlotte’s hand didn’t stop. “We’re not erasing you,” she whispered. “We’re ending you.”", 1, `The promises rose from the page in three bright strands. One curled around the Keeper’s outstretched hand, one crossed his cloak, and one reached the dark place where his face should have been. He struck at them, but honest words would not tear. Charlotte kept the pen moving while Emma read each sentence aloud and Lila guarded the page. The Keeper tried once more to command them, yet his voice broke into whispers before the words reached their ears. “We do not have to erase what happened,” Charlotte told him. “We choose what it means.”`);

  replaceKeeperPassageOccurrence(6, "The lantern flared, its flame burning brighter, casting long shadows across the walls. The attic roared, alive with twisted magic, but the girls didn’t stop. Their words glowed brighter, burning through the attic’s silence, rewriting the story itself. The Keeper’s shadow screamed, his voice echoing through the attic. Every ending is a beginning, he said, his voice fading. Charlotte’s hand didn’t stop. She wrote the final line: The ink sleeps. The pen fell silent, its glow fading, and the attic grew still.", 1, `Only one line remained. The Keeper stretched across the rafters, thin as smoke in morning light. Every ending is a beginning, he said, but the thunder had gone from his voice. Charlotte looked at Emma and Lila. Neither told her what to write; they did not need to. Their joined hands had already chosen it. Charlotte lowered the nib and formed the final words slowly enough for all three of them to read: The ink sleeps. The full stop shone like a tiny lock clicking closed. The pen fell silent, its glow fading, and the attic grew still.`);

