  addKeeperPassage(7, "“We did it,” she said softly.", `Her smile wavered. “I nearly made it worse,” she admitted. “When the whispers called me, part of me wanted to listen. I thought if I understood the Keeper first, I could be the brave one who fixed everything.”

  Charlotte sat beside her on the floor. “I hid the notebook because I thought I had to understand everything before I told you. That did not help either.”

  Emma lowered herself on Lila’s other side. “And I kept calling it tricks because facts felt safer than saying I was frightened.” She gave a tired little laugh. “Apparently we are all very talented at trying to do impossible things alone.”

  Lila leaned against them. “New rule, then. No secret plans with haunted writing tools.”

  “Very specific,” Emma said.

  “It needs to be.” Charlotte held out her hand. “No one comes into this attic alone. No one touches the pen alone. And if one of us hears a whisper, all three of us hear about it.”

  They stacked their hands over the fading marks and agreed. The rule could not seal every kind of magic, but it sealed the spaces between them where the Keeper had tried to grow.`);

  addKeeperPassage(7, "The ink slept, but only for now.", `Charlotte found a clean exercise book beneath a pile of maps. On its cover, Emma wrote THE ATTIC RECORD in large block letters. Lila added three small stars and, after a moment’s thought, a firmly closed chest. They began recording everything while the details were fresh: the mirror’s messages, the shape of the marks, the way honest sentences had resisted the Keeper, and the exact words of their final rewrite.

  They did not create the record because they wanted another adventure. They created it because forgetting would leave the next person unprepared.

  “Page one should be the rules,” Charlotte said.

  Emma numbered them carefully. Never investigate alone. Test what the magic shows you. Say fear aloud before it becomes a secret. Let everyone help choose the ending.

  Lila tapped the final rule. “And keep a lantern ready.”

  Emma added it with a smile.

  Emma kept the record open across her knees. Their account belonged to them, and it would stay in their own handwriting until every rule was recorded and every name was signed.`);

  addKeeperPassage(7, "Emma joined her, her voice trembling but hopeful. “And it’s ours.”", `Below the attic window, the neighbourhood began its morning in small, familiar sounds. A milk bottle clinked against a doorstep. A dog barked twice and then remembered it was still early. Curtains opened across the street, and a bicycle bell rang somewhere beyond the garden wall. After hours inside a room that had tried to become the whole world, those ordinary noises sounded like music.

  Lila opened the window. Cool air rushed in, smelling of wet leaves and toast from a nearby kitchen. It lifted their hair and sent the last harmless motes of dust dancing through the sunlight.

  “I forgot the world was this big,” she said.

  “The Keeper wanted the attic to feel like there was nowhere else,” Emma replied. “But there was always a window.”

  Charlotte watched sunlight travel across the closed book. Darkness had made every object seem like part of the Keeper’s plan. Morning returned their proper shapes. A chest was a chest. A mirror was glass. A blank page was a possibility, not an order.

  She breathed in deeply. The air no longer tasted of ink.`);

  addKeeperPassage(7, "The three girls stood together, their breaths steady, their hearts united.", `Before leaving, they returned to the Attic Record and added another page: what they would do if the ink woke again. First, find one another. Second, tell a trusted grown-up instead of carrying the danger in silence. Third, read their own account before believing any new message. Fourth, remember that a frightening sentence was not the same thing as fate.

  “We should sign it,” Lila said.

  Their three signatures crossed the bottom of the page. This time no living ink curled around the letters. The names remained simple and dark and wonderfully ordinary.

  Charlotte closed the record. “Now it’s ready.”

  “Ready,” Emma agreed, “and not forgotten.”

  Then they carried the chest to the centre of the room where none of the shadows could hide it. Charlotte wrapped the pen in plain linen and placed it inside. Emma closed the lid, checked the latch twice, and threaded a small bell through it—not because a bell could defeat magic, but because it could warn them if the lid moved. Lila kept the lantern raised while they worked, its steady light shining across the bell.`);

  addKeeperPassage(7, "The lantern flickered once more, then steadied, its flame burning strong.", `They finally allowed themselves to imagine the rest of the day. Warm drinks. Clean clothes. Breakfast large enough to make up for a night of battling a story. Sleep with open curtains and sunlight on the floor. The plans were small, but small plans felt precious after the Keeper’s enormous promises and threats.

  At the attic door, Lila looked back. “Do you think stories really sleep?”

  Emma considered it. “Maybe they rest inside the people who remember them.”

  “Then we decide how this one is remembered,” Charlotte said. “Not as the night the Keeper nearly won.”

  Lila’s smile returned, brighter this time. “As the night three brilliant heroes saved one another?”

  “Three extremely tired heroes,” Emma corrected.

  Their laughter was quiet at first, as if they had forgotten how. Then it filled the attic, warm and untwisted. Nothing copied it. Nothing turned it into a warning. It belonged only to them.`);

  replaceKeeperPassageOccurrence(7, "But even as the words left her lips, she felt the attic watching, its silence pressing down on her, reminding her that stories never truly end.", 1, "Even after they agreed, Lila could still feel the attic watching. The feeling no longer asked her to keep a secret; it reminded her to keep the rule.");

  replaceKeeperPassageOccurrence(7, `Emma sat down on the floor, her hands trembling as she traced the final words on the page. The ink sleeps. She whispered them aloud, her voice steady. “It’s resting,” she said. “We gave it an ending.” Charlotte nodded, her eyes blazing. “And when it wakes again, we’ll be ready.” Lila smiled faintly, her voice firm. “Together.”

The attic groaned softly, almost like a sigh — not of warning, but of peace. The lantern’s glow filled the room, banishing the shadows, warming the air. Charlotte felt the weight lift from her chest, her breath steadying. For the first time in weeks, she felt safe. The attic was quiet, the ink asleep, the Keeper gone.`, 1, `Emma bent over the Attic Record and traced the copied final words with one finger: The ink sleeps. “It’s resting,” she said. “We gave it an ending.”

Charlotte looked at the rules beneath the sentence. “And if it wakes again, we’ll know what to do.”

“Together,” Lila said.

Above them, the beams settled with a soft sigh—not a warning, only the old house easing towards morning. The lantern warmed their faces, and Charlotte felt the tightness leave her chest. The danger had passed for now, and they had kept both their story and one another safe.`);

  replaceKeeperPassageOccurrence(7, `The attic hummed faintly, almost like a heartbeat, but softer now, gentler. It wasn’t a warning. It was a promise. Charlotte smiled faintly, her voice steady. “The ink sleeps,” she whispered. “And so do we.” The lantern flickered once more, then steadied, its flame burning strong.`, 1, `A faint hum passed through the attic, softer than a heartbeat. Charlotte listened until it faded without forming words. “I think the attic is finally letting us leave,” she said. The lantern flickered once, then steadied, its flame warm and strong.`);

  replaceKeeperPassageOccurrence(7, "The book lay closed, the pen resting beside it, silent and still. The attic was quiet, the ink asleep, the Keeper gone. But the girls knew the truth — stories never truly end. They only wait for someone to write the next word.", 1, "The book lay closed beside the latched chest. Inside, the wrapped pen rested silent and still. But the girls knew the truth — stories never truly end. They only wait for someone to write the next word.");

  addKeeperPassageBefore(7, "But the girls knew the truth — stories never truly end. They only wait for someone to write the next word.", `They stepped onto the landing and pulled the door closed together. The new bell on the chest gave one faint, clear chime, then fell silent. Charlotte paused with her hand on the handle, listening. No scratching followed. No voice called her name.

  Downstairs, daylight spread across the hallway. Their shadows moved ahead of them—three separate shapes that met whenever they walked close. Charlotte no longer mistook those shadows for the Keeper. They were proof that light stood behind them.

  The completed Attic Record rested safely under Emma’s arm. The lantern swung from Lila’s hand. Charlotte carried the ordinary pencil they had used to begin it. They left the magical pen behind, but they did not leave the power of writing with it.

  After the girls had gone downstairs, a breeze slipped through the round attic window. It turned no pages and opened no locks. It merely stirred the dust around the chest into three small trails that ran side by side before fading.

  Morning light rested on the lid, and the small bell did not move.`);

